  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 258 AM CDT Thu Oct 22 2026\n\nUpper ridge continues to build into the central CONUS early this \nmorning while the closed low slowly moves onshore of the CA \ncoastline. As a result, sfc high pressure leaves clear skies \noverhead with light winds for many locations, although some (namely \nCNK) are staying better mixed than others. So far the river valley \nat TOP has been the main spot in our CWA that has observed shallow \nground fog, and would expect fog like this to be confined to the \ntypical low-lying spots more prone to it, keeping it isolated and \npatchy. This should burn off soon after sunrise with another warm \nday on tap. The upper ridge axis moves overhead today into Friday, \nand slightly deeper mixing today should also aid in temperatures \nbeing a touch warmer than yesterday. Southerly winds increase into \nFriday thanks to a lee sfc trough tightening the pressure gradient \nahead of the upper system, bringing gusts to 25-35mph across most of \nthe area. Assuming our high temperatures continue to verify around \nthe 90th percentile of guidance (no reason to expect differently \nwith this setup), most places should manage to reach the low 80s \neach day through the end of the week and possibly Sunday as well.\n\nMost guidance continues to be in general agreement with keeping the \ntrough's eastward progression slow enough to bring the associated \ncold front through the area sometime on Monday. It's still early for \nthe NAM, but it is a notable outlier in that it starts to bring the \nboundary into the area Saturday night. Other guidance has the front \nnearby, but keeps it northwest of the area and stalls it until \nMonday. There are still enough differences in timing and speed of \nthe front that wide temperature ranges remain Monday onward. Still, \nthe overall trend is for cooler temperatures into the 60s behind the \nfront and moderating closer to seasonal values through mid-week. \nRain chances associated with the front begin in north central KS \nSunday night and spread east into Monday, but remain highest in the \nwestern half of the CWA (30-70%). Some showers may linger into \nTuesday morning if the upper trough is still slower to progress east \nby that time.",
